  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "Compares benchmark accuracy at two model sizes in each of two model families, bearing on downstream performance with scale. It does not measure held-out pretraining loss or fit scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites Kaplan et al. as background but does not reproduce or evaluate their loss-scaling laws. The accuracy results bear on V6. The reasoning-advantage analysis and its first-error/EDI results concern qualitative reasoning patterns, not the loss, allocation, architecture, modality, or downstream-prediction measures in V1–V7; the two reported qualitative advantage findings are recorded under fits_no_version. Clustering-selection settings and EDI values are not results on a listed claim version.",
    "test_reason": "In a new setting (reasoning-model families on reasoning benchmarks), it reports benchmark accuracy at two sizes per family, which bears weakly on V6's claim that downstream performance rises with scale. This is a preliminary motivating experiment with only two sizes per family. No curve, loss relation or prediction is fitted, so it is not T2 for V6's predictability.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely right. The authors conclude that larger models in both families consistently show Constraint-Guided Reasoning. They also argue it is the primary capability gap, citing the smaller models' first-error analysis. The benchmark gains are presented as preliminary motivation, not as a scaling result.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study compares discrete model sizes using benchmark accuracy and analyzes paired reasoning traces; it does not fit held-out cross-entropy scaling laws or optimize parameter/token allocation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reasoning analysis is conditioned on the gap-based filter (performance gap ≥ 0.6), repeated inference (T = 10), and the selected clustering settings (Qwen3 d′ = 8, K = 6; GPT-OSS d′ = 4, K = 6).",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "LLM-extracted, clustered advantage descriptions suggest larger models use constraint-guided reasoning more often than smaller ones. This is restricted to questions with a pass-rate gap of at least 0.6.",
        "proposed_claim": "Larger same-family reasoning models' advantage on hard questions comes chiefly from constraint-guided reasoning rather than trial and error.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R23",
          "R24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Smaller models' first errors are consistent (mean EDI below 0.4 in every domain, Table 11). They concentrate at the Transformation and Process Skills depths, as labelled by an LLM extractor.",
        "proposed_claim": "Smaller models' failures on scale-gap questions are systematic and concentrated at problem-transformation and execution stages.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/lgyeee/Where Do Larger Model Excel",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "Only two model-family pairs and two sizes per family are compared. The qualitative trace analysis is filtered to questions with a performance gap of at least 0.6, and advantage extraction and cluster selection use LLMs. The study does not test loss-based predictability or scaling-law fits. Reported OlympiadBench uncertainties are very large for Qwen3, and GPT-OSS accuracy decreases slightly on physics OlympiadBench.",
    "rows_left_out": "none",
    "untested": [
